WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:04.387
A food plant wants less waste: a wish, not yet a goal.

2
00:00:05.494 --> 00:00:10.439
Write the goal in one sentence, with the client: from where, to where, by when,

3
00:00:10.625 --> 00:00:11.345
measured how.

4
00:00:12.428 --> 00:00:14.379
Read it back, in their words.

5
00:00:15.471 --> 00:00:20.579
Start with a baseline, the measured starting value, or every result gets disputed.

6
00:00:21.327 --> 00:00:26.134
Here: 3.2% scrap, 2,400 kilos a week.

7
00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:27.841
No data?

8
00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:31.592
Then two weeks of measuring is your first deliverable.

9
00:00:32.287 --> 00:00:35.143
I don't know the number yet, and neither do you.

10
00:00:36.115 --> 00:00:42.048
Goal: scrap from 3.2 to 2.0% by end of June, measured in the weekly scrap report.

11
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.373
Scope is what the work covers: start-up, machine settings, work instructions.

12
00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:52.482
What is out of scope matters more: the packaging supplier,

13
00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:56.325
the recipes, and what people will raise, to point to later.

14
00:00:57.368 --> 00:01:01.687
Scope creep: the work widens, one reasonable request at a time.

15
00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:04.095
Could you also look at line 2?

16
00:01:05.101 --> 00:01:10.488
Don't just say no: good idea, but out of scope; we swap, or the sponsor adds time.

17
00:01:11.162 --> 00:01:11.998
What do we drop?

18
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.334
Acceptance criteria: who signs off, on what evidence, when.

19
00:01:18.023 --> 00:01:22.586
Here the production manager, after four weeks in a row at 2.0% or less.

20
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.017
One good week can be luck.

21
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:30.575
Agree it at the start; by the end, everyone remembers it differently.

22
00:01:31.575 --> 00:01:35.232
Experts differ: a stretch target motivates some teams,

23
00:01:35.348 --> 00:01:38.471
but one nobody believes in gets quietly ignored.

24
00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:42.352
This goal saves 900 kilos a week.

25
00:01:43.695 --> 00:01:44.705
Now the breakdown.

26
00:01:45.415 --> 00:01:51.417
A grid operator wants last-week cancellations from 18 to under 8% in six months.

27
00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:55.941
Start with deliverables, not activities: what will exist,

28
00:01:56.103 --> 00:01:57.647
checkable, that isn't there now?

29
00:01:58.379 --> 00:01:59.099
Four to seven.

30
00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.868
With two planners and the team lead, four go on the wall: baseline,

31
00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:08.930
readiness check on permits, materials and crew, six-week pilot, handover.

32
00:02:09.956 --> 00:02:12.592
This makes a WBS: goal at the top,

33
00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:16.412
deliverables below, work packages with activities at the bottom.

34
00:02:17.408 --> 00:02:22.423
The 100% rule: the branches cover all the work, nothing outside it.

35
00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.202
What isn't in it, nobody does.

36
00:02:26.656 --> 00:02:30.731
One person or a small team can do and finish a work package.

37
00:02:31.729 --> 00:02:36.628
The 8–80 rule, a rule of thumb: under about 8 hours is micromanagement,

38
00:02:36.837 --> 00:02:40.460
over about 80 hides surprises, and can't be tracked.

39
00:02:41.543 --> 00:02:45.363
Each package gets a verb, an owner, and a definition of done,

40
00:02:45.525 --> 00:02:47.580
like the planning manager agreeing.

41
00:02:48.607 --> 00:02:54.436
Under the baseline: extract three months of data, 16 hours, the data analyst.

42
00:02:55.196 --> 00:02:57.808
Classify with two planners, 12 hours.

43
00:02:58.576 --> 00:03:01.259
Review, 4 hours: smaller, but fine.

44
00:03:02.557 --> 00:03:05.761
Break it down with the people doing the work: sticky notes,

45
00:03:05.889 --> 00:03:08.304
a column per deliverable, sixty minutes.

46
00:03:09.332 --> 00:03:11.758
People commit to what they helped break down,

47
00:03:11.921 --> 00:03:15.346
and spot what you forgot: brief the switching technicians.

48
00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:19.409
Often forgotten: data access, the works council,

49
00:03:19.548 --> 00:03:23.043
permissions, training the night shift, the handover to the line.

50
00:03:24.044 --> 00:03:25.066
Let's just start?

51
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:27.074
Agreed, this week.

52
00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:30.692
Give me one hour to see what we're signing up for.

53
00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:33.024
Don't understand the work?

54
00:03:33.751 --> 00:03:37.222
Ask the expert: what must be finished before you can start?

55
00:03:38.656 --> 00:03:39.736
Then estimating.

56
00:03:40.433 --> 00:03:45.611
Give a range: six to ten weeks is more honest than eight, and shows the uncertainty.

57
00:03:46.641 --> 00:03:50.751
A three-point estimate: optimistic, most likely and pessimistic.

58
00:03:51.796 --> 00:03:57.950
PERT, from 1958, counts the middle one four times and divides by six.

59
00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:03.665
4, 6 and 14 days gives 7, more than 6: more can go wrong than right.

60
00:04:04.806 --> 00:04:08.231
The planning fallacy: we estimate from the best case.

61
00:04:08.953 --> 00:04:13.597
Students expected 34 days for their thesis; it took 56.

62
00:04:14.627 --> 00:04:17.088
We forget the waiting and our normal job.

63
00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.430
Ask how long similar jobs actually took.

64
00:04:21.805 --> 00:04:25.207
Effort is hours of work; duration is calendar time.

65
00:04:25.915 --> 00:04:29.027
A shift lead may have 20% of his week free.

66
00:04:30.005 --> 00:04:34.684
Ask the real hours a week: 16 hours at 4 a week is four weeks.

67
00:04:35.384 --> 00:04:38.240
Holidays and peak season go on the duration.

68
00:04:39.242 --> 00:04:41.204
A buffer absorbs what goes wrong.

69
00:04:41.926 --> 00:04:45.989
Don't pad every task secretly: work expands to fill the time.

70
00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:50.776
Put one visible buffer at the end, or before a hard milestone.

71
00:04:51.465 --> 00:04:53.102
Half gone a quarter of the way in?

72
00:04:53.832 --> 00:04:54.900
An early warning.

73
00:04:56.191 --> 00:05:00.173
The maintenance engineer at a packaging plant says: three days.

74
00:05:00.912 --> 00:05:04.674
Don't argue; ask the best case, and what makes it longer.

75
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.393
Best 2 days, if the export works.

76
00:05:09.149 --> 00:05:13.398
Worst 10: the system was migrated, the old data may be messy.

77
00:05:14.374 --> 00:05:19.447
2 plus four times 3 plus 10, divided by six, is 4 days of work.

78
00:05:20.226 --> 00:05:23.581
One day a week: about 4 weeks, not 3 days.

79
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.739
In the plan: 2 to 10 weeks, most likely 4.

80
00:05:28.497 --> 00:05:32.248
After the first export, the sponsor hears which end it is.

81
00:05:33.257 --> 00:05:34.314
Don't know yourself?

82
00:05:35.047 --> 00:05:38.982
Give a range and what would narrow it, and a board the date you commit to.
